<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-chain-conditions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Chain conditions</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            We introduce in this section important classes of maodules, viz., Artinian and Noetherian modules. In this section we assume that <m>R</m> is a commutative ring with unity and <m>A</m> is a right <m>R</m>-algebra. For an <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m> we denote by <m>S(M)</m> the collection of all <m>A</m>-submodules of <m>M</m>.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <definition xml:id="def-artinian-module">
        <title>Artinian Module</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                An <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m> is said to be <term>Artinian</term> if there are no infinite, strictly decreasing sequences of submodules of <m>M</m>, i.e., if <me>
                    N_1\supsetneq N_2\supsetneq N_3\supsetneq\cdots
                </me>
                is a decreasing chain of <m>A</m>-submodules of <m>M</m> then, there exists <m>\ell\in\N</m> such that <m>N_i=N_j</m> for all <m>i,j\geq\ell</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <definition xml:id="def-noetherian-module">
        <title>Noetherian Module</title>
        
        
        <statement>
            <p>
                An <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m> is said to be <term>Noetherian</term> if there are no infinite, strictly increasing sequences of submodules of <m>M</m>, i.e., if <me>
                    N_1\subsetneq N_2\subsetneq N_3\subsetneq\cdots
                </me>
                is a increasing chain of <m>A</m>-submodules of <m>M</m> then, there exists <m>\ell\in\N</m> such that <m>N_i=N_j</m> for all <m>i,j\geq\ell</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
    <p>
        We often use the following equivalent conditions for Artinian/Noetherian modules.
    </p>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-existence-of-minimal-maximal-element">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Following are equivalent for an <m>A</m>-module <m>M</m>.
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The module <m>M</m> is Noetherian (resp. Artinian);
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Every nonempty subset of <m>S(M)</m> has a maximal (resp., minimal) element.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>
    <example xml:id="ex-noetherian-but-not-artinian">
        <title>Noetherian but not Artinian</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If we consider <m>\Z</m> as a <m>\Z</m>-module then, <m>\Z</m> is Noetherian but not Artinian.
            </p>
        </statement>
        
    </example>
    <example xml:id="ex-artinian-but-not-noetherian">
        <title>Artinian but not Noetherian</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If we consider <m>\Z(p^\infty)=\{a/p^n:a\in\Z,n\in\N\}</m> as a <m>\Z</m>-module then, <m>\Z</m> is Artinian but not Noetherian.
            </p>
        </statement>
        
    </example>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-technical-condition">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>P,Q,R</m> be <m>A</m>-submodules of <m>M</m> with <m>P\subseteq Q</m>. Then there exists the following exact sequence of <m>A</m>-modules.
                <me>
                    0\to Q\cap R\big/P\cap R\to Q/P\to Q+R\big/P+R\to 0
                </me>
                
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>
    <lemma xml:id="lem-artinian-noetherian-short-exact-sequence">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>0\to N\to M\to P</m> be a short exact sequence of <m>A</m>-modules. The module <m>M</m> is Noetherian (resp. Artinian) if and only if <m>N</m> and <m>P</m> are Noetherian (resp. Artinian).
            </p>
        </statement>
    </lemma>
</section>